        <item>
            <title>0.4.0</title>
            <pubDate>Sun, 08 Nov 2026 21:10:15 +0000</pubDate>
            <link>https://github.com/thaw-app/Floe/releases/tag/0.4.0-rc.1</link>
            <sparkle:channel>beta</sparkle:channel>
            <sparkle:version>9</sparkle:version>
            <sparkle:shortVersionString>0.4.0</sparkle:shortVersionString>
            <sparkle:minimumSystemVersion>26.0</sparkle:minimumSystemVersion>
            <description><![CDATA[<p><strong>macOS 26 and later · Build 9</strong></p>
<h3>New</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Run your scripts on a result.</strong> Add a <code class="notranslate">@floe.subjects</code> line to a script command, such as <code class="notranslate"># @floe.subjects file, folder</code>, and it shows at the end of the Actions menu (⌘K) of apps, files, folders or links instead of in the search. In Settings › Commands › Actions it can be what Return does, have its own keys, or be left out of the menu. The script is told what it acts on in environment variables, such as <code class="notranslate">FLOE_PATH</code> and <code class="notranslate">FLOE_NAME</code>. <a href="docs/INTERFACES.md#actions-on-results">INTERFACES.md</a> lists them all.</li>
<li><strong>Scripts can act on text.</strong> A script command with <code class="notranslate"># @floe.subjects text</code> is listed in the Actions menu (⌘K) of clipboard entries and snippets, and gets the text in <code class="notranslate">FLOE_TEXT</code>.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Changed</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Floe uses less memory until you search for an emoji.</strong> The emoji and symbol table is built the first time you type <code class="notranslate">:</code> and let go when Floe frees memory after you close it.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Fixed</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Large numbers are grouped the way your region writes them.</strong> With the Mac set to India, the calculator shows 10,00,002, not 1,000,002.</li>
<li><strong>A negative answer no longer has a comma after its minus sign.</strong> <code class="notranslate">0K to °F</code> was drawn as -,459.67 °F.</li>
</ul>]]></description>
